course: dictionary
lesson: 5189
title: Using context to understand an animal
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Context is the words around a new word, and it often gives clues to its meaning. «One meerkat stands guard as a sentinel, watching for eagles and jackals.» Eagles fly in the sky, so the jackal is probably an animal that is a danger to meerkats on the ground.
explain: The sentence «...alarm calls tell the others whether danger comes from the sky, like an eagle, or from the ground, like a jackal» gives an even clearer clue. The words «from the ground» show that a jackal is an animal that walks on the ground, not a bird.
explain: Check your guess by swapping in your meaning. Try «from the ground, like a wild dog» in the sentence. Does it still make sense? If it does, you have probably understood the word. If you are not sure, look it up in a dictionary.
fact: A jackal is a wild animal like a small, thin dog.
fact: Jackals live in Africa and Asia, and they eat small animals and leftovers from other hunters.
fact: In stories and fables, the jackal is often a sly trickster.
fact: In Ancient Egypt, the god Anubis was shown with a jackal's head.
fact: Informally, «jackal» can mean a sneaky person who takes advantage of others' work or misfortune.
exercise: «One meerkat stands guard as a sentinel, watching for eagles and jackals.» What is a jackal? | A large bird that hunts fish in rivers | A sort of cave where meerkats sleep | *A wild animal like a small, thin dog | A type of tall grass on the savanna
exercise: Egyptians worshipped gods shown with animal heads, such as «Anubis with a jackal's head». Which animal is Anubis shown with? | An eagle | A falcon | A scorpion | *A jackal
exercise: «...whether danger comes from the sky, like an eagle, or from the ground, like a jackal.» Which word tells you where a jackal is? | *Ground | Eagle | Sky | Danger
test: Jackals live in which two continents? | Europe and South America | *Africa and Asia | Australia and Antarctica | North America and Greenland
test: Informally, what can «jackal» mean? | A kind teacher who helps everyone | *A sneaky person who takes advantage of others' work or misfortune | A brave firefighter who rescues people | A fast runner in a race
exam: Jackals eat small animals and leftovers from other hunters. Which best describes what a jackal eats? | Only seeds and fruit | Only fish from rivers | Only grass and leaves | *Small animals and leftovers from other hunters
